# ⛔ ONE PATH GETS merge=union, AND ITS NARROWNESS IS THE POINT (card#7227).
#
# WHY. changelog-gate.yml REQUIRES a `[Unreleased]` entry from every PR touching an in-scope
# file, and every entry goes to the same anchor — the top of a `[Unreleased]` subsection — so
# every merge to dev leaves every open sibling PR CONFLICTING. Measured at 21 of the last 40
# first-parent commits touching docs/CHANGELOG.md, against six concurrent worktrees. The cost
# is not only the resolution: a conflicted PR gets ZERO CI runs, because GitHub schedules no
# `pull_request` workflow when it cannot compute the merge commit, so its check set is
# `total_count: 0` — neither green nor red, and a naive "any failures?" read answers *no*.
#
# ⛔ DO NOT ADD CLAUDE_DECISIONS.md, AND DO NOT ADD docs/config-schema.md. Not caution —
# both were measured being destroyed by this driver on real conflicts (#552/#553):
#   * CLAUDE_DECISIONS.md needs NUMERIC DL ordering (DL-294 before DL-295 before DL-296).
#     Union interleaves by position and produces the wrong order silently.
#   * docs/config-schema.md had ONE long table row edited by both sides (DL-294's
#     either-family wording and DL-296's archived-twin clause). Union emits the row TWICE;
#     picking a side drops a shipped decision. It needed both semantic additions spliced into
#     one row by hand — no automatic rule could have produced that.
# A conflict in either of those, and in every other path here, still stops for a human.
#
# TWO RESIDUALS, measured, neither gated. (a) Union is SILENT, and applies to every three-way
# merge git performs — merge, rebase, cherry-pick and revert — not only to a PR merge. Two
# consequences, each measured with its no-attribute control: a commit that REWORDS an existing
# entry, cherry-picked onto a branch that appended a new one, leaves BOTH spellings, stale copy
# included (control: that one conflicts); and `git revert` of a commit whose entry sits beside a
# later one becomes a NO-OP — `nothing to commit, working tree clean`, rc 1, entry still in the
# file — because union re-adds exactly what the reverse patch removes (control: a conflict, ALSO
# rc 1, so the exit code does not distinguish them; read the message). A merged `[Unreleased]` is
# never presented for review, so read the merge commit's diff of it.
# (b) ORDER FOLLOWS MERGE DIRECTION. With a release fold (`[Unreleased]` → `[X.Y.Z]`) as OURS
# and a feature branch as THEIRS, the incoming entry lands UNDER the version heading — clean,
# well-formed and wrong. Merge dev INTO the feature branch; never a feature branch into
# release/v<version>. Re-cut the release branch instead.
docs/CHANGELOG.md merge=union
